+Defines the training pipeline for Splatt3R, a feed-forward model that predicts 3D Gaussian primitives directly from uncalibrated stereo image pairs. It distinguishes itself from pixelSplat by eliminating explicit pose regression overhead and using a specific loss masking strategy to prevent hallucinating geometry in occluded regions.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** pixelSplat accelerates rendering by 2.5 orders of magnitude compared to state-of-the-art light field transformers [S1]. Compared to the next-fastest baseline, the cost to infer a single scene (encoding) and then render 100 images is about 650 times less [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** pixelSplat uses significantly less memory per ray at training time compared to baselines [S1].
-*   **Performance:** Benchmarked on wide-baseline novel view synthesis on the real-world RealEstate10k and ACID datasets [S1]. It outperforms baselines like pixelNeRF and GPNR, producing higher perceptual quality (lower LPIPS) and avoiding ghosting artifacts caused by incorrect depth predictions found in models without epipolar encoding [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Real-time and memory-efficient rendering; fast 3D reconstruction at inference time; explicit 3D representation that is editable; cheap to train [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Struggles with reflective surfaces (transparency) and out-of-distribution views (billboard-like artifacts) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Inference cost is approximately 650 times less than the next-fastest baseline for encoding one scene and rendering 100 images [S1]. Rendering is accelerated by 2.5 orders of magnitude compared to state-of-the-art light field transformers [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Memory-efficient rendering due to rasterization-based volume rendering of Gaussians, significantly less memory per ray at training time compared to ray-marching NeRFs [S1].
+*   **Performance:** Benchmarked on wide-baseline novel view synthesis on RealEstate10k and ACID datasets. Outperforms baselines like pixelNeRF, GPNR, and Du et al.'s light field transformer in PSNR and LPIPS [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Real-time rendering, editable 3D representation, fast 3D reconstruction at inference, handles scale ambiguity, avoids local minima via differentiable sampling [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Struggles with reflective surfaces (transparency) and out-of-distribution views (billboard effect) [S1]. Requires paired images with known poses (though scale is handled) [S1].
 
 ## Background
-pixelSplat was introduced by Charatan, Li, Tagliasacchi, and Sitzmann from MIT and Simon Fraser University/University of Toronto [S1]. It was presented at CVPR 2024 [S1]. The method addresses the limitations of generalizable novel view synthesis by integrating 3D Gaussian primitives, which allow for real-time rendering and interpretable 3D structure, into a feed-forward framework [S1].
+pixelSplat was introduced by Charatan, Li, Tagliasacchi, and Sitzmann from MIT and Simon Fraser University/University of Toronto [S1]. It was presented at CVPR 2024 [S1]. The method addresses the limitations of previous generalizable novel view synthesis methods, which were either slow (ray-marching NeRFs) or lacked explicit 3D representations (light-field transformers) [S1]. It builds on the success of 3D Gaussian Splatting for single-scene optimization [19] and adapts it for the generalizable, feed-forward setting [S1].
 
